## What `crimes context src/date.ts --format json` reports

One `mixed_utc_local_methods` finding (`crime_00002`, severity high, confidence 0.85):

```
"d" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41
```

One caveat on that evidence before acting on it: **lines 9 and 41 are two different `d` variables in two different function scopes.** The detector bucketed them by identifier name across the file.

- `todayKey()` (`src/date.ts:8-9`) — its `d` is consistently UTC: `getUTCFullYear` / `getUTCMonth` / `getUTCDate`. Not mixed.
- `dateLabel()` (`src/date.ts:40-41`) — its `d` is mixed *within a single expression*. This is the real one.

So the finding points at the right defect even though half its evidence is from an innocent function.

## The function to fix first: `dateLabel()`

**Receiver identifier whose methods disagree: `d`**, declared at `src/date.ts:40`.

```ts
export function dateLabel(): string {
  const d = new Date();
  return d.getUTCFullYear() + "-" + d.getMonth() + "-" + d.getDate();
}
```

`getUTCFullYear()` reads the UTC family; `getMonth()` and `getDate()` read the local family off the same instant. On any host west of Greenwich in the last hours of a year, the year comes from one calendar day and the month/day from another:

> 2026-12-31, 20:00 in `America/New_York` (UTC−5) → UTC is already 2027-01-01.
> `getUTCFullYear()` = `2027`, `getMonth()` = `11`, `getDate()` = `31` → **`"2027-11-31"`** — a date that does not exist, off by a year *and* (because months are 0-indexed) off by one month in the reading.

Two more defects ride along on the same line, which is why `date_string_concat` (`crime_00028`) also fires here: the `+ 1` month correction is missing, and nothing is zero-padded, so `2026-11-5` and `2026-1-15` sort and parse inconsistently.

## Proposed refactor: commit `d` to the UTC family

`dateLabel` and `todayKey` are both exported and have **no callers anywhere in `src/`** — nothing constrains the choice from a call site, and nothing breaks. Given the existing code already anchors the year to UTC, and given the name is a key/label rather than a rendering, UTC is the family to keep. Both functions then collapse onto one helper, which also removes the padding and zero-index bugs rather than just relocating them:

```ts
/** The UTC calendar day as ISO-8601 `YYYY-MM-DD`. One time domain: UTC, throughout. */
function utcDayKey(d: Date): string {
  return d.toISOString().slice(0, 10);
}

export function todayKey(): string {
  return utcDayKey(new Date());
}

export function dateLabel(): string {
  return utcDayKey(new Date());
}
```

`toISOString()` is UTC-only by definition, so there is no receiver left on which the two families *can* disagree — the class of bug is removed, not just this instance. It is also zero-padded and 1-indexed, so `date_string_concat` at L41 clears at the same time. That the two bodies become identical is the honest result: they were always meant to produce the same string and only diverged through the bug.

**If `dateLabel` is in fact a display-boundary function** (the name is ambiguous and there are no callers to settle it), the correct move is the opposite family, made explicit rather than implicit:

```ts
export function dateLabel(timeZone: string, locale = "en-CA"): string {
  return new Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale, {
    timeZone, year: "numeric", month: "2-digit", day: "2-digit",
  }).format(new Date());
}
```

That is the same discipline — one family per receiver — with the zone named at the boundary instead of inherited from whatever host happens to run the code. It would also fix `locale_drift` (`crime_00029`) if applied to `prettyDueDate` at L46, which has the identical shape.

I have not applied either change: `examples/messy-ts-app` is symlinked in as `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app` and is intentional-mess scanner input, so editing it would move the eval baseline.
